WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.304
Welcome!

00:00:01.304 --> 00:00:04.561
In this Java tutorial, you'll use AutoCloseable

00:00:04.561 --> 00:00:07.679
and try-with-resources to connect an object's

00:00:07.679 --> 00:00:11.004
cleanup to its lifetime, then trace the order in

00:00:11.004 --> 00:00:13.568
which Java closes multiple resources.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:17.590
Reliable cleanup keeps files and other limited

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.163
resources available for later work.

00:00:20.163 --> 00:00:23.692
When an application handles many requests, tying

00:00:23.692 --> 00:00:27.147
cleanup to scope helps prevent one request from

00:00:27.147 --> 00:00:30.234
leaving resources unavailable to the next.

00:00:30.878 --> 00:00:34.160
Today we'll build a small classroom model for

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.764
campus workshop staff.

00:00:35.764 --> 00:00:38.973
Two named objects will report when their use

00:00:38.973 --> 00:00:40.140
begins and ends.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.640
These printed messages model resource lifetimes;

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.140
they do not borrow real equipment or open actual

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:47.578
files.

00:00:48.228 --> 00:00:51.166
We expect the program to acquire the first

00:00:51.166 --> 00:00:54.314
resource, acquire the second, and perform its

00:00:54.314 --> 00:00:54.663
work.

00:00:54.663 --> 00:00:57.601
Then Java should close the second resource

00:00:57.601 --> 00:01:00.889
before the first, and only afterward print that

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:02.428
the operation is done.

00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:06.535
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:06.535 --> 00:01:07.337
Main.java.

00:01:11.059 --> 00:01:12.293
The file is open.

00:01:12.293 --> 00:01:15.632
We need each object to identify itself when it

00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:17.083
starts and finishes.

00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:20.349
Let's define NamedResource, connect it to the

00:01:20.349 --> 00:01:23.833
AutoCloseable interface, and give it a field for

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:24.559
that name.

00:01:31.222 --> 00:01:33.977
The name field belongs to each object.

00:01:33.977 --> 00:01:37.166
Implementing AutoCloseable promises that our

00:01:37.166 --> 00:01:39.341
class supplies a close method.

00:01:39.341 --> 00:01:42.458
The interface gives Java a standard cleanup

00:01:42.458 --> 00:01:45.720
operation to call, but this declaration alone

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.692
does not create an object or schedule its

00:01:48.692 --> 00:01:49.272
cleanup.

00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:52.259
Next, the constructor will store the name

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:55.381
supplied by the caller and report that this

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:57.632
object's modeled use has begun.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:09.714
The parameter brings the caller's name into the

00:02:09.714 --> 00:02:10.554
constructor.

00:02:10.554 --> 00:02:13.493
This dot name selects the field on the new

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:16.572
object, so that name remains available after

00:02:16.572 --> 00:02:17.482
construction.

00:02:17.482 --> 00:02:20.701
The Opened message makes the beginning of this

00:02:20.701 --> 00:02:22.450
modeled lifetime visible.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.927
Now let's provide the promised close operation.

00:02:25.927 --> 00:02:29.100
It will report the end of use with the same

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:29.985
stored name.

00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:40.759
Override asks Java to check that this method

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:43.880
fulfills the inherited method relationship.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.767
It does not execute close.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:49.105
Our close method prints a lifecycle message; a

00:02:49.105 --> 00:02:52.443
real resource would perform its actual release

00:02:52.443 --> 00:02:53.169
work here.

00:02:53.169 --> 00:02:56.435
The constructor and close method use the same

00:02:56.435 --> 00:02:59.483
field, so their messages identify the same

00:02:59.483 --> 00:02:59.991
object.

00:03:00.005 --> 00:03:03.324
With our model ready, let's add the Main class

00:03:03.324 --> 00:03:04.767
and its main method.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:08.015
This is the program's entry point when we run

00:03:08.015 --> 00:03:09.530
the Java application.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:17.826
The main method will own this operation.

00:03:17.826 --> 00:03:20.959
We want both resources to finish their use when

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:24.026
the operation ends, so we'll declare them in a

00:03:24.026 --> 00:03:27.159
try-with-resources header instead of relying on

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:30.159
manual close calls at the bottom of the work.

00:03:43.727 --> 00:03:47.195
Java initializes resources from left to right.

00:03:47.195 --> 00:03:50.738
It constructs first, then second, and both must

00:03:50.738 --> 00:03:54.281
initialize successfully before the body begins.

00:03:54.281 --> 00:03:57.371
The declarations inside these parentheses

00:03:57.371 --> 00:03:59.407
register automatic cleanup.

00:03:59.407 --> 00:04:02.422
The semicolon separates the two resource

00:04:02.422 --> 00:04:03.402
declarations.

00:04:03.454 --> 00:04:06.715
Let's give the body a Work message, then put

00:04:06.715 --> 00:04:10.050
Done after the entire try statement so we can

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.754
see where cleanup fits.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.498
Work runs while both resources are available.

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:28.649
When the body finishes, Java calls close in

00:04:28.649 --> 00:04:31.726
reverse initialization order: second, then

00:04:31.726 --> 00:04:32.166
first.

00:04:32.166 --> 00:04:35.537
Done is outside that scope, so it follows both

00:04:35.537 --> 00:04:36.783
close operations.

00:04:36.783 --> 00:04:40.300
Let's click Run and check the complete sequence.

00:04:45.363 --> 00:04:48.048
The two Opened lines show first being

00:04:48.048 --> 00:04:49.935
constructed before second.

00:04:49.935 --> 00:04:52.765
Work appears only after both are ready.

00:04:52.765 --> 00:04:56.248
The Closed lines reverse that order, with second

00:04:56.248 --> 00:04:57.918
finishing before first.

00:04:57.918 --> 00:05:01.401
Done appears last because continuation waits for

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:02.707
cleanup to finish.

00:05:02.707 --> 00:05:05.900
We did not put any manual close calls in the

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:06.263
body.

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:09.071
The order comes from the positions in the

00:05:09.071 --> 00:05:12.106
resource header, not from the names we chose.

00:05:12.106 --> 00:05:15.276
Let's keep the variables and declaration order,

00:05:15.276 --> 00:05:17.704
but swap the names passed to the two

00:05:17.704 --> 00:05:18.581
constructors.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.043
Before running, predict all six output lines.

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:38.113
The first variable now refers to the object

00:05:38.113 --> 00:05:41.469
named second, and the second variable refers to

00:05:41.469 --> 00:05:43.111
the object named first.

00:05:43.111 --> 00:05:46.539
Which name will appear in each Opened and Closed

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:47.110
message?

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:50.180
Pause here and follow the header positions.

00:05:58.232 --> 00:06:01.986
Let's click Run and compare that prediction with

00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:04.332
the actual lifecycle messages.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.596
Opened now shows second before first, because

00:06:12.596 --> 00:06:15.823
those are the names supplied in initialization

00:06:15.823 --> 00:06:16.243
order.

00:06:16.243 --> 00:06:19.470
Closed shows first before second, because Java

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.416
still closes the later resource before the

00:06:22.416 --> 00:06:23.257
earlier one.

00:06:23.257 --> 00:06:25.712
Work and Done keep their positions.

00:06:25.712 --> 00:06:28.728
Changing a name changed the report, not the

00:06:28.728 --> 00:06:29.640
cleanup rule.

00:06:29.692 --> 00:06:32.787
You connected an object's close operation to

00:06:32.787 --> 00:06:36.163
scope with AutoCloseable and try-with-resources.

00:06:36.163 --> 00:06:39.398
The output showed when each lifetime began and

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:42.423
ended, and confirmed reverse closing order.

00:06:42.423 --> 00:06:45.166
This pattern lets the code describe the

00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:48.542
operation while Java arranges cleanup around it.

00:06:48.602 --> 00:06:51.927
For a transfer question, imagine the work raises

00:06:51.927 --> 00:06:54.975
an exception after both resources are ready.

00:06:54.975 --> 00:06:57.885
Which close operations should Java attempt

00:06:57.885 --> 00:07:01.002
before a handler responds, and in what order?

00:07:01.002 --> 00:07:04.327
Use the resource lifetimes we just traced as you

00:07:04.327 --> 00:07:07.652
prepare for the notebook's failed-path examples.
